SOURCE @ahmadi2021
    description: Evaluates social equity impacts of interconnected energy-water transition scenarios in Iran's southern coast using distributive justice indicators.
    epistemic_model: Social equity theory, distributive justice, energy-water nexus planning
    method: Quantitative scenario evaluation using proxy indicators (subsidies, taxation, job creation, emissions, environmental concerns)
END SOURCE

ITEM @ahmadi2021
    text: This study aims to assess the impacts of these transition plans on social equity in a case study of the southern coast of Iran, where the community faces severe water scarcity and there is ready access to seawater for desalination.

    note: Water scarcity creates necessary condition for desalination adoption, enabling transition pathway evaluation

    chain: Water_Scarcity -> ENABLES -> Desalination
END ITEM

ITEM @ahmadi2021
    text: The main contribution of this research is to consider social equity as a key factor in the nexus evaluation of energy-water transition plans.

    note: Positions equity as essential evaluation criterion, constraining acceptable transition configurations

    chain: Equity -> CONSTRAINS -> Transition_Planning
END ITEM

ITEM @ahmadi2021
    text: In order to achieve more realistic and sustainable water-energy supply planning, the study focuses on a quantitative distributive justice evaluation of the proposed scenarios, with consideration of related social equity theories and Iran's particularities, using the following proxy indicators: subsidies, taxation, job creation, NOx emissions, as well as environmental concerns.

    note: *complex* Five-indicator convergence (subsidies, taxation, employment, emissions, environment) operationalizes justice evaluation across economic, social, environmental domains

    chain: Subsidy -> INFLUENCES -> Justice

    note: Taxation mechanism redistributes transition costs, directly affecting justice outcomes

    chain: Taxation -> INFLUENCES -> Justice

    note: Employment generation distributes economic benefits, shaping justice perceptions

    chain: Employment -> INFLUENCES -> Justice

    note: Emissions distribution creates environmental burden disparities affecting justice outcomes

    chain: Emission -> INFLUENCES -> Justice

    note: Environmental impacts create burden distribution affecting justice evaluation

    chain: Environment -> INFLUENCES -> Justice
END ITEM

ITEM @ahmadi2021
    text: In the proposed scenarios with decentralized desalination, shifting a portion of job creation and subsidies for desalination and their renewable energy supply from urban areas and reallocating them to rural areas led to a balance in the distribution of benefits and burdens between rural and urban households, while addressing the issue of excluding rural households from the benefits and opportunities provided by renewable-powered desalination projects.

    note: Reveals spatial redistribution mechanism: decentralization enables geographic reallocation of benefits, which enables equity balance

    chain: Decentralization -> ENABLES -> Benefit_Distribution -> ENABLES -> Equity
END ITEM
